= Cará (Yam)

_Scope: This entry treats_ cará _(Brazilian Portuguese) and the_ Dioscorea _yam genus broadly._ Cará _in southern and southeastern Brazilian usage refers principally to_ Dioscorea alata _L. (water yam, white yam, ñame, purple yam) and related African / Asian Dioscorea species (_D. cayennensis Lam._,_ D. rotundata Poir._); in some regional Brazilian usage_ cará _and_ inhame _are interchanged (see_ inhame.typ _for the_ Colocasia esculenta _taro discussion and the regional-naming complexity). The_ Chinese yam _—_ Dioscorea polystachya _Turcz. (= D. opposita Thunb., D. batatas Decne.) — 山藥 (shānyào) — is a_ canonical classical Chinese Bencao drug _and is treated separately in this entry as the Chinese-medical anchor; this is a distinct species from the Brazilian cará, but the dravyaguna profile and clinical register overlap substantially. Status: the Asian/African Dioscorea cultivars are classical food crops with deep South Asian, African, and Pacific traditional use; D. polystachya is canonical in Chinese medicine (_Shennong Bencao Jing _top grade); not classical in Tibb or Greco-Roman traditions in the strict Avicennan / Dioscoridean sense. NOTE: Brazilian "cará" is_ NOT _the same as the (Andean) potato (Solanum tuberosum) or the sweet potato (Ipomoea batatas — see_ sweet-potato.typ_)._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin / botanical:* The yam genus *_Dioscorea_* L. (family Dioscoreaceae) contains ~600 species, with several being major food crops:
- *_Dioscorea alata_ L.* — *water yam*, *winged yam*, *purple yam*, *ñame morado* (Spanish), *cará* / *cará-roxo* (Brazilian Portuguese); the most-widely-distributed cultivated yam globally; pan-tropical.
- *_Dioscorea cayennensis_ Lam.* — *yellow yam*, West African origin; major West African staple.
- *_Dioscorea rotundata_ Poir.* — *white yam*, West African origin; together with _D. cayennensis_ provides ~95% of West African yam production (Nigeria, Ghana, Benin, Côte d'Ivoire being the major producers; Nigeria produces ~70% of global yam supply).
- *_Dioscorea polystachya_ Turcz.* (= _D. opposita_ Thunb. = _D. batatas_ Decne.) — *Chinese yam*, *nagaimo*, *yamaimo*; the *canonical classical Chinese drug 山藥 (shānyào)*; the only Dioscorea genuinely classical in East Asian medicine.
- *_Dioscorea japonica_ Thunb.* — *Japanese yam*; closely related to D. polystachya; used in Japanese cuisine (nagaimo, yamaimo distinctions).
- *_Dioscorea bulbifera_ L.* — *air potato*; widely distributed; *toxic if not properly prepared* — boiling and processing required.
- *_Dioscorea trifida_ L.f.* — *cush-cush yam*, *yampi*; Amazonian / South American Dioscorea; one of the few American-domesticated yams.

The genus name *_Dioscorea_* is a Linnaean honor of *Pedanius Dioscorides* (1st c. CE, Cilician Greek physician, author of *_De Materia Medica_*) — appropriately commemorating the great classical pharmacognosist in the name of a genus he did *not* describe. The species epithet *alata* = Latin "winged," for the four-angled / four-winged stem of D. alata.

*Brazilian Portuguese:* *Cará* — from the Tupi-Guarani indigenous name; in southeastern and southern Brazilian usage refers to *Dioscorea* species, particularly _D. alata_ and _D. cayennensis_. In northeastern Brazilian usage *inhame* and *cará* are often interchanged or applied inversely (cará = taro, inhame = yam) — *the regional-naming complexity is real and a source of culinary confusion across Brazilian regions*. The Tupi *karaá* / *ká-rá* is the indigenous source. *Cará-roxo* — purple yam (the anthocyanin-rich D. alata cultivar). *Cará-do-ar* — air potato (D. bulbifera).#footnote[Brazilian regional yam / taro nomenclature: Houaiss, _Dicionário Houaiss_; standard Brazilian ethnobotanical literature; the cará / inhame regional inversion is documented in regional Brazilian agricultural and culinary literature. The Tupi-Guarani indigenous origin of "cará" reflects the pre-Columbian Tupi cultivation of native and possibly some early-introduced yams.]

*Spanish:* *Ñame* — pan-Hispanic Caribbean and African; from West African / Pulaar *ñami* "to eat" — transmitted to Spanish via Atlantic slave-trade African languages. The word *yam* in English is *also* from the same African source (Pulaar/Wolof/Fulani *nyami*), via Caribbean English colonial usage.

*Chinese:* *山藥 (shānyào)*, "mountain medicine" — the canonical Chinese drug name for *_Dioscorea polystachya_*, recorded in the *Shennong Bencao Jing* (神農本草經, Han dynasty) as a *top-grade drug (上品)*. The classical name also *薯蕷 (shǔ yù)* — the older form. *淮山 (huái shān)*, "Huai mountain" — for the high-quality material from the *Huaiqing* region of Henan province (modern Jiaozuo / Wenxian district); the *Huai shan / huaishanyao* designation is comparable to a geographical-quality appellation (Champagne, Min-County dang gui). The Japanese reading is *yamaimo* (山芋) — the same characters, though Japanese culinary distinctions go further: *nagaimo* (長芋, "long yam") for elongated cultivars; *jinenjo* (自然薯, "natural yam") for the wild D. japonica.

*Sanskrit:* The classical Indian register has multiple Sanskrit names for yam-class root crops:
- *आलू (ālu)* — used broadly for yam-and-tuber-class roots; in classical context, often refers to Dioscorea rather than the modern Hindi *aloo* (which now means *Solanum tuberosum* / potato — a *post-Columbian terminology shift*, where the Old World Sanskrit/Hindi name for yam was transferred to the New World potato after the latter's introduction).
- *रत्नालू (ratnālu)*, *क्षीरालू (kṣīrālu)*, *पिण्डालू (piṇḍālu)* — specific named yam varieties.
- *कन्द (kanda)* — generic "root / tuber" category; classical Ayurvedic kanda-varga.
- *विधारा (vidhārā)* — sometimes for specific Dioscorea species.

The classical Ayurvedic _kanda-varga_ treats yams generally as a sweet-cooling-snigdha class; specific species are recognized but not always sharply distinguished by classical commentators.#footnote[Sanskrit yam nomenclature: Monier-Williams; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Kanda-varga_; P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, _ālu_ entry. The post-Columbian transfer of *ālu* / *aloo* from yam to potato is one of the cleaner naming-displacement examples in South Asian botanical history.]

*Arabic / Tibb:* *No classical Avicennan entry for the major Dioscorea cultivars*. The herb is post-classical or non-classical in the strict Tibb sense. The African Dioscorea species are classical in West African ethnomedicine (Igbo, Yoruba, Akan traditional medicine systems) but outside the canonical Tibb literature. Modern Unani has minor entries for some Dioscorea species but the classical Avicennan register is essentially absent.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

*山藥 (shānyào)* — _Dioscorea polystachya_ — is *top-grade in the Shennong Bencao Jing* and is one of the most clinically central foods-medicines of the Chinese pharmacopoeia. The clinical register is *Spleen-Lung-Kidney qi-and-yin tonifying with neutral character* — making it one of the broadest-applicability tonic herbs.#footnote[山藥 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (top grade); elaborated in _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Neutral (平).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘 gān).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Spleen (脾), Lung (肺), Kidney (腎).

*Actions & indications:*
- *補脾養胃 (bǔ pí yǎng wèi)* — tonifies the Spleen, nourishes the Stomach — for Spleen-qi vacuity with poor appetite, fatigue, loose stools, weak digestion.
- *生津益肺 (shēng jīn yì fèi)* — generates fluids, augments Lung — for chronic cough, asthma, dry throat with Lung-yin vacuity.
- *補腎澀精 (bǔ shèn sè jīng)* — tonifies the Kidney, astringes essence — for seminal emission, spermatorrhea, urinary frequency, leukorrhea.
- *Wasting-thirst (消渴 xiāokě, the classical category overlapping diabetes mellitus):* 山藥 is a *cornerstone classical drug for xiāokě*, with the canonical Zhang Xichun formulas using massive doses (60–120 g) of 山藥 for diabetes; modern Chinese clinical use in type 2 diabetes is substantial and supported by some clinical evidence.
- *Modern register:* immune-supporting (the *yam polysaccharides — DOP*), hypoglycemic, hepatoprotective, gastroprotective.

*Cautions:* damp-heat patterns with excess phlegm; food stagnation; concurrent acute febrile illness. The neutral-sweet-tonifying register has *very few classical contraindications*, making 山藥 one of the safest and most widely-applicable tonic herbs.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

*Ālu / kanda* (आलू / कन्द) — classical Ayurvedic recognition of the yam class. The reading is *madhura rasa* (sweet), *guru-snigdha guṇa*, *śīta vīrya* (cooling), *madhura vipāka*. The classical Ayurvedic register on *kanda-varga* (root/tuber group) treats yams as *bṛṃhaṇa* (anabolic), *balya* (strengthening), *Vāta-pitta-śāmaka* — with the caveat that yams are *guru* (heavy) and may be inappropriate in deep Kapha excess or low _agni_. The specific classical attestations are less elaborately developed than for grains (śūka-dhānya) or pulses, but the kanda-varga register is preserved.#footnote[Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Kanda-varga_; Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (in the broader food-classification context); P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, _ālu_ and _kanda-varga_.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

*Not classical in Tibb.* The herb is not in the Avicennan canon; the West African Dioscorea staples are outside the geographical reach of the classical Arabic-Islamic medicine. Modern Unani has minor entries for some species. *No classical attestation; no Qurʾānic provenance*.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

*山藥 appears in numerous classical formulas:*
- *六味地黃丸 (Liù Wèi Dì Huáng Wán, "Six-Ingredient Rehmannia Pill")* — *the foundational Chinese yin-tonifying formula*; 熟地黃 + 山茱萸 + 山藥 + 澤瀉 + 茯苓 + 牡丹皮. From Qian Yi's _Xiao Er Yao Zheng Zhi Jue_ (Song dynasty); arguably the most-prescribed yin-tonifying formula in Chinese medicine, used for Kidney-yin vacuity broadly.
- *參苓白朮散 (Shēn Líng Bái Zhú Sǎn, "Ginseng, Poria, Atractylodes Powder")* — *the cornerstone Spleen-qi-tonifying formula with damp resolution*; 山藥 is a principal supporting ingredient.
- *理中湯 (Lǐ Zhōng Tāng)* and other Spleen-tonifying formulas — frequent inclusion of 山藥.
- *玉液湯 (Yù Yè Tāng, "Jade Liquid Decoction")* — Zhang Xichun's late-Qing wasting-thirst formula; *very large dose of 山藥* (sometimes 60–120 g) for diabetes-class xiāokě.
- *Single-herb dietetic preparations:* 山藥粥 (mountain medicine congee), 山藥湯 (yam soup), 蒸山藥 (steamed yam) — daily-tonic preparations.

=== Japanese (nagaimo, yamaimo)

- *Tororo (とろろ)* — *grated raw nagaimo* — eaten as a slimy topping over rice (麦とろ mugitoro), buckwheat (とろろそば tororo soba), or as a side dish. The mucilaginous texture is distinctive Japanese culinary aesthetic.
- *Yamakake* — tororo over sashimi.
- *Nagaimo no su-no-mono* — vinegared yam salad.

=== Ayurveda, Greco-Roman, Tibb

No major classical formulary tradition for the canonical Indian / Western / Tibb register. Brazilian / African / Caribbean use is *non-classical-with-deep-folk-traditional register*.

=== Brazilian / African / Caribbean

- *Cará cozido* — boiled yam; the canonical Brazilian preparation.
- *Pirão de cará* — Brazilian yam pirão (porridge).
- *West African yam preparations:* *fufu* (pounded yam — Nigerian, Ghanaian); *iyan* (Yoruba pounded yam); *yam porridge*; *grilled yam*; *yam pottage*. *Yam is the central staple of West African cuisine* in the yam belt (Nigeria, Ghana, Benin, Côte d'Ivoire, Togo, Cameroon).
- *Caribbean preparations:* boiled yam (canonical breakfast staple in Jamaica, Trinidad, other islands), yam-and-saltfish, yam pies, yam soups.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

The yam genus *Dioscorea* is *pan-tropical* — adapted to *warm, frost-free, moist or seasonally-moist* conditions. Different species occupy different climatic niches: *D. polystachya* (Chinese yam) tolerates *warm-temperate-to-subtropical* conditions; *D. alata* (water yam) is pan-tropical; *D. cayennensis* and *D. rotundata* are West African tropical; *D. trifida* is Amazonian. See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework.

=== Hot-humid tropical (West African yam belt — the central food-staple climate)

The West African yam belt has the most-elaborated yam-as-staple tradition globally. *Yam is the central calorie source* in Nigerian, Ghanaian, and adjacent cuisines; the *New Yam Festival* (Iwa-ji, Igbo) and parallel Yoruba and Akan festivals are *major cultural-religious events* — yam is invested with *deep cultural and spiritual significance* in West African traditions.

*Cares.* The sweet-snigdha-cooling profile is climatically apt for the hot-humid tropical climate as a basic staple; classical West African preparation (boiled, pounded into fufu, served with soup) is the established traditional pattern.

=== Continental seasonal-extreme (Chinese, Japanese, Korean — the medicinal register)

*山藥 (shānyào) / yamaimo* is the *medicinal-and-dietetic* yam — different species from the West African staples but related register. Climatic use is *all-season tonic* in Chinese medicine, with particular emphasis on cool-season Spleen-Lung-Kidney support.

=== Hot-humid tropical Brazilian (cará register)

The Brazilian cará register is post-colonial: the African (D. alata, D. cayennensis) and Asian-introduced cultivars were established through the Atlantic slave-trade African transmission and the broader Columbian-exchange agricultural diffusion. Modern Brazilian cará is dietetically established across the country.

=== Modern transposition

Modern globalized health-food register has spread yam consumption beyond traditional zones, with the orange-fleshed and purple-fleshed cultivars receiving particular attention for antioxidant content.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Approximate composition (raw Dioscorea alata, per 100 g):*
- Energy ~118 kcal; Water ~70 g
- Carbohydrate ~28 g (starch predominant); fiber ~4 g
- Protein ~1.5 g; Fat ~0.2 g
- *Minerals:* potassium very high (~816 mg — among the highest of any common food), manganese, copper, modest other minerals
- *Vitamins:* vitamin C (modest), B-vitamins (thiamin, B6, folate)
- *Anthocyanins* — significant in purple-fleshed D. alata cultivars; antioxidant register
- *Yam polysaccharides (especially DOP from D. polystachya)* — immunomodulatory and gastroprotective preclinically
- *Diosgenin* — *the famous steroidal saponin* — central in *industrial steroid pharmaceutical synthesis*; *Dioscorea* species (particularly D. composita and Mexican wild yam D. mexicana) were the original commercial source of diosgenin for the synthesis of progesterone and the *first oral contraceptive pill* (the *Marker degradation* by Russell Marker, 1940s, using Mexican Dioscorea). The food-yams contain modest diosgenin but the steroid-synthesis industry uses specific wild Mexican species.
- *Glycemic index:* low-to-moderate; favorable diabetic-dietary register
- *Allergenic potential:* low; rare allergy reports#footnote[Composition data: USDA FoodData Central; standard nutritional databases; diosgenin and steroidal saponin chemistry in standard pharmacognosy. The Marker degradation / contraceptive-pill historical synthesis from Mexican wild yam is one of the great 20th-c. natural-product chemistry stories.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Immune-supportive (DOP polysaccharides)* — preclinical and modest clinical.
- *Hypoglycemic* — modest; supports the classical xiāokě register.
- *Cholesterol-lowering* — modest.
- *Gastroprotective* — preclinical.
- *Diosgenin* — the *commercial steroidal precursor*; *food-yam diosgenin content does NOT cause hormonal effects in normal dietary consumption*; the *Western "wild yam cream for hormonal balance" marketing claim is unsupported* — the human body does not convert dietary diosgenin to progesterone or estrogen. *The wild-yam-cream / topical-hormone claims popular in some menopause supplements are scientifically unfounded* — flagged.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Culinary register:* unrestricted at normal dietary intake.
- *Chinese decoction (山藥):* 9–30 g per day; up to 60–120 g in specific traditional formulas (Zhang Xichun's xiāokě regimens).
- *Raw nagaimo (Japanese):* the mucilaginous proteinase can occasionally cause oral irritation in sensitive individuals.
- *D. bulbifera (air potato):* *requires proper preparation* — *toxic if eaten raw or improperly cooked*; not casually consumed.
- *Pregnancy:* generally safe at dietary intake.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dose ranges: standard nutrition literature; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. for the Chinese clinical dose; Zhang Xichun, _Yi Xue Zhong Zhong Can Xi Lu_ for the high-dose xiāokē formulas.]

